# The Dzhanibekov effect: a torque-free brick spun about its INTERMEDIATE
# axis, which is the one axis of the three about which steady rotation is
# unstable.
#
# A cuboid of half-extents (0.5, 1, 2) has three distinct principal
# moments, so all three of Euler's equations are coupled. Spin it almost
# exactly about the middle one -- w = (1e-4, 3, 1e-4), a spin of 3 with a
# perturbation ten thousand times smaller -- and the perturbation does
# not stay small. The body periodically flips end over end.
#
# Nothing about that violates conservation. WITH NO TORQUE THE ANGULAR
# MOMENTUM L IS EXACTLY CONSTANT, and so is the rotational energy; it is
# the angular VELOCITY that swings around, because for a body whose three
# moments differ, w and L do not point the same way. This is the clearest
# case where carrying L in the state vector rather than w is what makes
# the conservation exact rather than approximate.
set system.g_constant = 0
collide off
new cuboid as brick { mass = 3, half_extents = [0.5, 1, 2], position = [0, 0, 0], angular_velocity = [1e-4, 3, 1e-4] }
method adams
energy
angmom
get brick.angular_velocity
run 25 steps 25
angmom
energy
get brick.angular_velocity
norm(brick.orientation)
